## Appendix C: Proofs for the Policy

### C.1 Approximation Bound

**Proposition C.1 (Chapter 9, Prop. 9.10).** If \(|V(a) - \hat{V}(a)| \le \varepsilon\) for all \(a \in P_t\), and \(\hat{a} = \arg\max \hat{V}\), then

\[
V(\hat{a}) \ge \max_a V(a) - 2\varepsilon.
\]

**Proof.** Let \(a^* = \arg\max V(a)\). Then

\[
V(\hat{a}) \ge \hat{V}(\hat{a}) - \varepsilon \ge \hat{V}(a^*) - \varepsilon \ge V(a^*) - 2\varepsilon = \max_a V(a) - 2\varepsilon.
\]

\(\square\)

### C.2 Finite Stopping

**Proposition C.2 (Chapter 9, Prop. 9.12).** If \(C(a) \ge \varepsilon_C > 0\) for all \(a\), then \(T \le \lceil B/\varepsilon_C \rceil < \infty\).

**Proof.** Each action costs at least \(\varepsilon_C\). After at most \(\lceil B/\varepsilon_C \rceil\) actions, the cumulative cost satisfies \(\sum C(a_i) \ge B\), triggering the stopping condition. \(\square\)

### C.3 Diminishing Returns

**Proposition C.3 (Chapter 9, Prop. 9.13).** If \(\mathbb{E}[\Delta U \mid a] \le \delta_t\) for all \(a\) and \(\delta_t \to 0\), and \(\varepsilon_C > \beta N_{\max} + \varepsilon_V\), then \(\max_a V(a) < \varepsilon_V\) for sufficiently large \(t\).

**Proof.** For any action \(a\),

\[
V(a) = \mathbb{E}[\Delta U \mid a] - C(a) + \beta N(a) \le \delta_t - \varepsilon_C + \beta N_{\max}.
\]

If \(\varepsilon_C > \beta N_{\max} + \varepsilon_V\), then

\[
V(a) < \delta_t - \varepsilon_V.
\]

As \(\delta_t \to 0\), there exists \(t_0\) such that \(\delta_t < \varepsilon_V\) for all \(t \ge t_0\). Then \(V(a) < 0 < \varepsilon_V\). Since this holds for all \(a\), \(\max_a V(a) < \varepsilon_V\). \(\square\)

### C.4 UCB Finite-Sample Exploration

**Proposition C.4 (Chapter 9, Prop. 9.6).** With UCB bonus \(N(a) = \sqrt{2\log t / n_t(a)}\) and finite budget \(B\), every action in \(P_0\) is selected at least once before the budget is exhausted, provided \(\beta\) is sufficiently large.

**Proof.** Initially, \(n_0(a) = 0\) for all \(a \in P_0\). Define \(N(a) = +\infty\) when \(n_t(a) = 0\) (or use a large constant). The first action \(a_1\) is selected arbitrarily. After selection, \(n_1(a_1) = 1\), while \(n_1(a) = 0\) for all \(a \ne a_1\). Therefore \(N(a) = +\infty\) for all \(a \ne a_1\), and the next action must be one of these (since \(\beta \cdot \infty > \text{any finite } V\)). By induction, all actions in \(P_0\) are selected before any action is repeated. Since \(P_0\) is finite with \(|P_0|\) elements, all actions are selected in the first \(|P_0|\) steps. As long as \(B \ge |P_0| \cdot \varepsilon_C\), the budget suffices. \(\square\)

### C.5 UCB Regret

**Proposition C.5 (Chapter 9, Prop. 9.7).** With UCB and infinite budget, the number of times a suboptimal action is selected is \(O(\log B)\).

**Proof.** This is the standard UCB regret bound (Auer, Cesa-Bianchi, and Fischer, 2002). For \(K = |P_t|\) actions and horizon \(T\), the regret is \(O(\sqrt{KT \log T})\), so the number of suboptimal selections is \(O(\sqrt{KT \log T})\) in the worst case and \(O(K \log T / \Delta^2)\) when the gap \(\Delta\) between optimal and suboptimal is bounded away from zero. For finite budget \(B\) with costs bounded below by \(\varepsilon_C\), the horizon is \(T \le B/\varepsilon_C\), giving \(O(K \log B)\) suboptimal selections. \(\square\)

